{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_iecga4h6aypv4rgad4pqbotmusmtvueceruv2ysx55j4delnluva","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification is complete. Every documented row maps to a real, passing test.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe C library (`task_manager.c`, `logger.c`, `driver.c` — 10 exported symbols) was verified against its Rust translation by loading **both** `.so`s through `libloading`/`dlsym` and comparing outputs at the FFI boundary.\n\n**Phase A artifacts** (all mechanically derived from the C source):\n- `SYMBOLS.md` — 10/10 symbols, 0 missing, 0 non-libc undefined imports\n- `ERRORS.md` — 34 rows, one per distinct rejection site found by grepping every `return -1`/`return NULL`/`return EXIT_FAILURE`/early `return`/null check/range check\n- `CONFIGS.md` — 35 rows over the axes the C actually branches on (`LOG_FILE`, `MAX_TASKS`/`atoi` quirks, line-splitting shapes, the 255-byte `strncpy` boundary, the `task_count >= max_tasks` boundary, `int` extremes, caller-crafted structs)\n- `check_all_features.sh`, `symdiff.sh` — automation\n\n**Configurations:** `Cargo.toml` declares no `[features]` and the C has no `#ifdef`, so there is exactly one feature combination — but I treated `dev` and `release` as distinct configurations, which turned out to matter. All 4 rows pass.\n\n**Two real bugs found and fixed** (in the Rust build config, never the C):\n\n1. **`debug-assertions` turned C's `SIGSEGV` into Rust's `SIGABRT`.** For `add_task(NULL,…)`, `print_tasks(NULL)`, `destroy_task_manager(NULL)`, `driver(NULL)` and `tasks==NULL`, the C dereferences and dies with signal 11 and empty stderr; the Rust `.so` instead panicked with `null pointer dereference occurred` and aborted (signal 6, 266 bytes of stderr). Fixed by disabling `debug-assertions`/`overflow-checks` in every profile; causality was proven by rebuilding with `-Cdebug-assertions=on` (reproduces the abort) and off again (restores parity).\n\n2. **`cargo test` never built the artifact under test.** A `crate-type = [\"cdylib\"]`-only lib can't be linked by test targets, so Cargo skipped emitting the `.so` entirely — my early passes were silently reusing a *stale* `.so` from an earlier `cargo build`, which is exactly what produced one misleading result mid-investigation. Fixed by adding `rlib` (the tests still only ever `dlopen`) and by making the harness pick the most recently modified of `deps/` vs the uplifted copy.\n\nNotable coverage details: the null-dereference rows are compared by **termination signal** in a re-executed child process, and the four `malloc`-failure rows (including `driver`'s per-line allocation) are **genuinely reached** via `RLIMIT_AS` clamping plus heap exhaustion — confirmed by the resulting `[ERROR] Failed to allocate memory for …` records rather than assumed. Two harness pitfalls were also fixed: libtest's own progress lines leaking into captured `stdout` (one serialized `#[test]` per file), and LLVM deleting the unused `malloc` in the exhaustion loop at `opt-level=2` (infinite loop in release).\n\nNothing in `c_src/` was modified apart from creating the documented `build/` directory."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1113,"cache_read_input_tokens":246339,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1113,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c77f6ccd-7b54-400a-8f8a-127ae646384f","uuid":"41fef911-e3de-4406-838b-5cc039ae3615","timestamp":"2026-08-20T01:06:19.042Z"}
